const HelpBuiltins = "  The weft shell being a dialect of bash with a sandboxed and slimmed down\n  coreutils, a lot of it should come automatically for you. Otherwise, why else\n  would you be here? And a handful of commands --  cd, wc, history,\n  clear, which, rev, tac, exit -- are indeed indistinguishable from\n  their real counterparts. Most have limitations and/or differences. Of course we\n  have a handful of builtins that are weft's special sauce. An overview of all of\n  them are below.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mDifferences and limitations\x1b[0m\n  Only the options listed here are supported. Any other option is rejected with\n  an error (grep: unknown option -o, exit status 2). The exception is less,\n  which accepts options and ignores them.\n\n  Where a command can write depends on the directory. /log is read-only. /tmp is open to every member. /bin needs the binmgr group, /cron.d needs cronmgr, and /etc/dashboards and /etc/notify.yaml need configmgr (/etc/passwd and /etc/usage are read-only for everyone). Expiry, size limits and how files cross into /bin are in Scratch space (man tmp).\n\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand  Supported              Unsupported             Differences\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------  ---------------------  ----------------------  ----------------------\x1b[0m\n  >, >>    Redirect into a file   Redirecting into /log\n                                  or /etc/passwd\n  awk      Near complete          gensub, strftime,       Metavariables (man\n           implementation of One  systime                 metavars) are\n           True AWK                                       accessible in programs\n                                                          in constant casing\n                                                          (LEVEL, HOST, etc.)\n  cat      —                    All options             Reads /log pseudofiles\n                                                          as well as /tmp.\n  comm     -1 -2 -3               Other options\n  cp       —                    -r and other options    Files only, not\n                                                          directories. Copying\n                                                          between /tmp, /bin,\n                                                          /cron.d and the /etc\n                                                          config files triggers\n                                                          the destination's save\n                                                          checks.\n  cut      -d, -f                 -c -b -s --complement\n  date     -u, -d STRING, -I,     Other options\n           -R, +FORMAT\n  diff     diff <a> <b>           -u -q -y and other      Prints - and + lines\n                                  options                 rather than a unified\n                                                          diff.\n  find     [path...], -name,      -exec, -delete, -size,\n           -iname, -path, -type   -mtime, boolean\n           f|d, -maxdepth N,      operators\n           -mindepth N\n  git      log, show, diff,       add, rm, mv, stash,     Version history for\n           blame, status,         reset, clean,           /bin, /cron.d,\n           rev-parse, restore     branches, merges,       /etc/dashboards and\n           (--source), checkout   rebases, clone, fetch,  /etc/notify.yaml only.\n           <rev> -- <path>,       pull, push; flags       One linear branch\n           revert, commit         beyond the ones each    where every save is a\n           --amend -m             command lists in man    commit, so there is no\n                                  git                     staging. Only the last\n                                                          5 past versions of\n                                                          each file are kept.\n                                                          --author and --grep\n                                                          take RE2 patterns.\n                                                          Writes (restore,\n                                                          revert, commit\n                                                          --amend) need the\n                                                          group that manages the\n                                                          file. See man git.\n  grep     -i -v -c -n -E -q -l   -o -w -F -A -B -C -m\n           -h -H -r               -e -x -P -s\n  head     -n N, -N               -c\n  join     -t SEP, -1 N, -2 N,    -v -o -e -i             Matches rows with a\n           -a 1|2                                         hash, so input needn't\n                                                          be sorted (GNU join\n                                                          requires it). Rows\n                                                          with too few fields\n                                                          for the key are\n                                                          skipped.\n  jq       -r, -c, -s             -R -n -e -S --arg       Metavariables (man\n                                                          metavars) accessible\n                                                          in the form $level,\n                                                          $host, $ts, etc.\n  less     —                    All options (accepted,\n                                  no effect)\n  ls       -l -a -h               -R -t -r -S -1 -d -F\n  mkdir    -p                     Other options           /tmp only.\n  mv       —                    All options             Same rules apply as\n                                                          for cp\n  paste    -d                     -s\n  rm       -r, -R, -f             Other options\n  sed      s///, y///, d, p, a,   -E, -s                  Line filters (-n\n           G, N, addresses and                            '/re/p', '/re/d', and\n           ranges, -n, -e                                 their ! forms) are\n                                                          optimized (man\n                                                          fast-pipelines); s///\n                                                          and other programs run\n                                                          post-retrieval.\n  sort     -r -n -u -k -t SEP     -f -h -V -s -b,\n                                  character offsets in\n                                  keys (-k1.2)\n  tail     -n N, -N, -n +N, -f    -c, -F                  -f works on files\n                                                          under /log only and\n                                                          can't be redirected\n  tee      -a                     Other options\n  touch    —                    All options             /tmp only.\n  tr       tr SET1 SET2, tr -d    -s -c, [:class:]\n           SET, ranges like a-z\n  type     —                    All options\n  uniq     -c -d -u               -i -f\n  vi(m)    —                    —                     Edits anything you may\n                                                          write, with the same\n                                                          groups as >.\n  xargs    -n N, -P N, -I{}       -0 -d\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mWeft's own commands\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;208mQuerying\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand                   What it does               Reference\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m------------------------  -------------------------  -------------------------\x1b[0m\n  range [<start>..<end>]    Show or set the time       man range\n                            window every read uses.\n                            Relative specs like 1d..\n                            are re-anchored on every\n                            query.\n  explain <pipeline>        Say how much of a          man explain\n                            one-liner is optimized\n                            retrieval and what would\n                            speed it up. Runs\n                            nothing. See Making\n                            pipelines fast (man\n                            fast-pipelines).\n  trace, trace --id <id>,   The recent traces, every   Tracing (man trace)\n  trace --spans             line of one request\n                            across all your services,\n                            or a per-span rollup (of\n                            one request with --id, or\n                            of the whole time window\n                            without).\n  chart                     Render a pipeline's        chart (man chart)\n                            output, or a cron's\n                            history, as a chart in\n                            the terminal.\n  alert                     Fire a notification from   alert (man alert),\n                            a pipeline's final         notifications (help\n                            number.                    alerts)\n  weft::latest, weft::avg,  Read and reason about      weft::\\* (man crons)\n  weft::max, ...            your own cron history\n                            from inside a pipeline.\n  dashboard                 Open a dashboard; the      dashboard (man dashboard)\n                            YAML grammar and tile\n                            kinds.\n  clipboard                 Copy a short, shareable    man clipboard, man\n                            link to a /bin script.     sharing\n\n  See man less for paging and man sharing for sharing scripts.\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mSharing and history\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand  What it does  Reference\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m-------  ------------  ---------\x1b[0m\n\n  \x1b[38;5;208mTeam\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8mCommand                     What it does                 Reference\x1b[0m\n  \x1b[38;5;8m--------------------------  ---------------------------  ---------------------\x1b[0m\n  adduser, deluser, groups,   Manage org members and       man passwd, man teams\n  id                          groups, not\n                              operating-system accounts.\n                              adduser <email> sends an\n                              invite. /etc/passwd is a\n                              read-only team roster.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mFinding help\x1b[0m\n  help lists the shell's topics, help builtins (or man commands) shows this page, and\n  man with no argument lists the manual. <command> --help shows the same\n  page as man <command>.\n"

// HelpCron — Crons (generated from docs/shell/help-cron.md).
const HelpCron = "\x1b[38;5;208mCrons\x1b[0m\n  A cron runs a pipeline on a schedule. Its stdout is recorded\n  as the cron result; writes to stderr are treated as alert firings\n  (if the cron has a | alert stage — see help alerts).\n\n  - vi /cron.d/<name> — create or edit a cron\n  - cat /cron.d/<name> — view a cron definition\n  - ls /cron.d — list existing crons; ls -l /cron.d/ adds a sparkline column\n  - chart <name> — chart recent results (man chart)\n  - rm /cron.d/<name> — delete a cron (stops the schedule)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mCron file format\x1b[0m\n    # schedule: every 1m\n    cat /log/myco/prod/api/main.log | grep ERROR | wc -l | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  # schedule: takes every <duration> or a five-field expression such\n  as */5 * * * *. Notification targets come from the alert stage's\n  --notify=<alias>. Each run reads only the logs from its own\n  schedule interval, so count with no row limit. Do not use head -n or\n  tail -n in a cron: they can cut the interval short.\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mAccessing past results\x1b[0m\n  weft::latest, weft::avg, weft::max, weft::min, weft::sum,\n  weft::stddev, and the rest of the weft::* family read a cron's\n  history; pass the cron's name, e.g. weft::latest error-rate. Windows\n  are durations like 30m, 1h or 1d (aggregates reach back 24 hours).\n  The predicates (weft::is_above, weft::is_outlier, etc.) return\n  exit codes for composing alerts. Full reference: man crons.\n\n    echo $(weft::avg error-rate 1h) | alert above 50 --notify=ops\n\n  (Here error-rate is the name of one of your own crons.)\n\n\x1b[38;5;208mTier limits\x1b[0m\n  {{TIER_LIMITS}}\n"

